When $\Delta n_{g}$ i.e. difference in gaseous moles of product and reactant is zero, then pressure has no effect and it is equilibrium point for reaction.

\begin{aligned}
&\mathrm{N}_{2}(g)+\mathrm{O}_{2}(g) \rightleftharpoons 2 \mathrm{NO}(g) \\
&\Delta n_{g}=2-(1+1)=0
\end{aligned}

$\therefore$ Pressure has no effect for this reaction.

For the following reaction,

\begin{gathered}
\mathrm{N}_{2}(g)+3 \mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{~g}) \rightleftharpoons 2 \mathrm{NH}_{3}(g) \\
\Delta n=\text { number of moles of product in gaseous state } \\
-\text { number of moles of reactant in gaseous state } \\
=2-(3+1)=-2 \\
\because \quad K_{p}=K_{C}(R T)^{\Delta n}
\end{gathered}

$\therefore \quad K_{p}=K_{C}(R T)^{-2}$
